1947021484375, -0.0152435302734375, 0.04095458984375, 0.040283203125, 0.0236968994140625, 0.01788330078125, 0.0015630722045898438, 0.031494140625, 0.025177001953125, -0.045196533203125, -0.00083160400390625, 0.01045989990234375, 0.03497314453125, 0.0106353759765625, -0.01299285888671875, 0.023040771484375, -0.00666046142578125, -0.036651611328125, 0.037078857421875, 0.01006317138671875, -0.002532958984375, -0.00911712646484375, -0.027587890625, 0.0027484893798828125, -0.012298583984375, -0.0095672607421875, -0.032379150390625, 0.0219268798828125, -0.00189208984375, 0.043975830078125, 0.00797271728515625, -0.0233612060546875, -0.007602691650390625, 0.0003161430358886719, 0.01030731201171875, 6.735324859619141e-06, 0.0008082389831542969, 0.0022068023681640625, -0.0145263671875, 0.01311492919921875, -0.01434326171875, 0.00849151611328125, 0.017425537109375, -0.0172882080078125, -0.00010883808135986328, 0.024169921875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent research has begun to investigate the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, especially in the analysis of electrical circuits (for examples and foundational approaches, see [1]\u2014use of GA in circuits, [2]\u2014GA in control, and [3]\u2014multivectors in engineering design). *Geometric Algebra* is a mathematical framework based on a real vector space of dimension \\( n = p + q \\), equipped with a metric of signature \\((p, q)\\): that is, with \\(p\\) basis vectors whose squares are \\(+1\\) and \\(q\\) whose squares are \\(-1\\).\n\nA *blade* is the exterior (wedge) product of \\( r \\) orthonormal basis vectors, and its *grade* is \\( r \\). *Multivectors* are general elements of the algebra \\(\\mathcal{G}_{p,q}\\), containing linear combinations of blades of different grades (for example, a multivector might be the sum of a scalar, a vector, and a bivector). Every multivector can be uniquely decomposed as \\( M = \\sum_{r=0}^n \\langle M \\rangle_r \\), where \\( \\langle M \\rangle_r \\) denotes its grade-\\( r \\) part. This framework allows complex physical quantities to be represented as combinations of scalars, vectors, bivectors, etc., providing unified tools for their manipulation.\n\n---\n\n### Motivation\n\nA central tool in systems analysis is the Laplace transform, which converts time-domain ordinary differential equations (ODEs) into algebraic equations in the frequency (or Laplace) domain. This enables analysis of stability, system behavior, and controller design via frequency response and algebraic solution techniques. Traditionally, Laplace transforms are defined only for real- or complex-valued functions; recently, extensions have been explored for certain hypercomplex algebras (e.g., quaternions [4], split-complex numbers [5]), but these are limited in scope.\n\n**Key limitations of existing extensions:**\n- They either work only for scalar or for specific algebra-valued functions\u2014not for arbitrary multivector functions (i.e., functions mixing grades).\n- Most require commutative multiplication (as in [5], which studies split-complex functions), or only partially address algebras with noncommutative products (such as [4] on quaternions).\n- They generally target low-dimensional or particular algebraic cases.\n\n---\n\n### Central Research Gap\n\n**There is presently no rigorous framework defining the Laplace transform for arbitrary multivector-valued functions**\u2014that is, functions mapping time to an element of \\(\\mathcal{G}_{p,q}\\), possibly with mixed grade content and subject to noncommutative multiplication rules. This prevents direct application of Laplace-domain analysis to physical systems whose natural state or signal variables are represented as full multivectors (e.g., combined current, flux, or field quantities in GA-based models).\n\n---\n\n### Objectives and Approach\n\nThis work aims to resolve the above gap by:\n1. Formally defining the Laplace transform for arbitrary multivector-valued functions \\( M(t): \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\), for signatures up to \\( n = 5 \\).\n2. Proving that fundamental properties\u2014such as linearity, the differentiation rule, and invertibility\u2014hold grade-by-grade, while correctly accounting for possible noncommutativity in GA multiplications.\n3. Demonstrating, via explicit examples, how this extension enables unified Laplace-domain analysis of physical systems previously beyond the reach of classical or previous hypercomplex Laplace theory.\n\n**These objectives directly address the research gap:** by making the Laplace transform available for GA-valued time signals, we enable frequency-domain analysis and control design for a much broader class of systems in physics and engineering.\n\n---\n\n### Background and Technical Definitions\n\n**Prerequisites:** We assume multivector-valued functions \\( M(t): [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) are piecewise continuous and absolutely integrable on every finite interval, i.e., \\( \\int_0^T \\| M(t) \\|\\,dt < \\infty \\) for all \\(T > 0\\), where \\( \\| \\cdot \\| \\) is any norm induced by the underlying vector space. These are the minimal standard regularity and integrability conditions ensuring the usual properties of the Laplace transform apply gradewise.\n\n**Laplace Transform Definition:**  \nGiven \\( M(t) = \\sum_{r=0}^n \\langle M(t) \\rangle_r \\), we define its Laplace transform for complex frequency variable \\( s \\) (with \\(\\text{Re}(s)\\) large enough for convergence) as\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{M\\}(s) := \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} M(t)\\,dt = \\sum_{r=0}^n \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\langle M(t) \\rangle_r\\,dt\n\\]\n**(Integration is performed separately for each grade-\\( r \\) component, just as scalar Laplace integrals.)** Here, scalar multiplication by \\( e^{-st} \\) precedes the (possibly noncommutative) geometric algebra product; the scalar factor acts by scaling, not by geometric multiplication, ensuring that *the Laplace integration itself does not introduce or alter the algebra\u2019s inherent noncommutativity*.\n\n**Invertibility Note:** The transform is invertible provided the scalar Laplace transform is invertible on every component grade; global invertibility thus reduces to gradewise invertibility. No new obstructions are introduced by the noncommutative structure because the transform does not mix grades or reorder products.\n\n---\n\n### Main Theoretical Results\n\n**Theorem 1 (Linearity):**  \nFor absolutely integrable multivector-valued functions \\(M(t), N(t)\\) and scalars \\(a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}\\),  \n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{a M(t) + b N(t)\\}(s) = a \\mathcal{L}\\{M(t)\\}(s) + b \\mathcal{L}\\{N(t)\\}(s)\n\\]\nThis follows grade by grade from the linearity of scalar integration.\n\n**Theorem 2 (Differentiation):**  \nIf \\( M(t) \\) is differentiable and both \\( M(t) \\) and \\( M'(t) \\) are absolutely integrable on \\( [0, \\infty) \\), then\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\frac{dM}{dt}(t)\\right\\}(s) = s \\mathcal{L}\\{M(t)\\}(s) - M(0)\n\\]\nwith \\( s \\in \\mathbb{C} \\) acting as a scalar multiplier, commuting with all multivector grades. The geometric (possibly noncommutative) product arises only when multiplying transformed signals.\n\n**Caveat:** Equations using \\(s\\) assume that \\(s\\) is scalar and thus always commutes with GA elements; additional care is needed if \\(s\\) is generalized.\n\n**Noncommutativity in Transform Calculations:**  \nSuppose \\(A, B \\in \\mathcal{G}_{p,q}\\), \\( M(t) = f(t) \\), \\( N(t) = g(t) \\) are scalar functions. The sum \\( AM(t) + BN(t) \\) is a multivector function. The differentiation property yields\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\Big\\{\\frac{d}{dt}(A f(t) + B g(t))\\Big\\}(s)\n= A\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{f'(t)\\}(s) + B\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{g'(t)\\}(s)\n= A \\left[s\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}(s) - f(0)\\right] + B \\left[s\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{g(t)\\}(s) - g(0)\\right]\n\\]\nHere, the products between algebra elements and the transformed (scalar) functions are geometric products; generally, for noncommuting elements, \\( A\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}(s) + B\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{g(t)\\}(s) \\neq (A+B)\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)+g(t)\\}(s) \\). This clarifies how noncommutativity is preserved throughout the Laplace-domain expressions.\n\n---\n\n### Examples\n\n**Example 1: RLC Circuit (Mixed Grades)**  \nSuppose an RLC circuit is described in geometric algebra with a current expressed as \\( I(t) = I_0(t) e_1 + \\Phi(t) e_{23} \\), where \\( e_1 \\) is a grade-1 (vector) basis element (representing current direction), and \\( e_{23} \\) is a grade-2 (bivector) basis element (representing, for example, magnetic flux orientation) in \\(\\mathcal{G}_{3,0}\\). Then,\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{I(t)\\}(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{I_0(t)\\}(s)\\,e_1 + \\mathcal{L}\\{\\Phi(t)\\}(s)\\,e_{23}\n\\]\nThis allows the simultaneous Laplace-domain analysis of both current and flux dynamics\u2014enabling, for example, frequency-domain synthesis of controllers or analysis of coupled dynamical effects that mix vector and bivector"}}